STEP 1 -- baseline, the telnet server is OFF (designed by the tester)      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: read `show telnet`, then try three telnet connections from the box while the
  server is disabled.  A capture on eth3 records what the DUT answered on the wire.
  Expected: `show telnet` reads Disabled, and every connection gets a RST.

awplus# show telnet
  Telnet Server Configuration
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Telnet server                      : Disabled
  Protocol                           : None
  Port                               : 23
awplus# show running-config | include telnet
  no service telnet

box$ tools/tb capture tb277 ckmgmt eth3 25 t33378-off.pcap tcp port 23

  Three connection attempts, all from the box, all while the server was Disabled:

box$ tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager
       --password friend --login-timeout 12
  -> TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.34:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused   (exit 4)
box$ (the same command again, 10.38.22.34)
  -> TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.34:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused   (exit 4)
box$ (the same command to the vlan48 SVI, 10.38.22.66, which leaves the box by eth4)
  -> TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.66:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused   (exit 4)

box$ tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt tcpflow /tmp/ckmgmt/t33378-off.pcap --port 23
  file /tmp/ckmgmt/t33378-off.pcap: 4 packet(s) total, 2 TCP flow(s) on port 23

  flow 10.38.22.33:57706 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=2  duration=0.000s
       => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
     10.38.22.33:57706 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
     10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57706  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}

  flow 10.38.22.33:57708 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=2  duration=0.000s
       => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
     10.38.22.33:57708 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
     10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57708  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}

  The 10.38.22.66 attempt left the box by eth4, so it is not in an eth3 capture.  Its
  exit code 4 (ECONNREFUSED) is the record for it.
  Third instrument: the DUT logged NO `telnetd ... Connection from` line for any of the
  three attempts.
  => STEP 1 PASS: with the server off, the DUT answers TCP/23 with a RST from both of its
     SVIs.  The RST comes from the kernel because no program is listening.  The telnet
     daemon never saw a connection, so this is a stronger baseline than silence.

STEP 2 -- enable the IPv4 telnet server (designed by the tester)           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: send `service telnet ip`, then read the state back.
  Expected: the command is accepted, `show telnet` reads Enabled / IPv4 / 23, and the
  configuration is stored.

awplus# configure terminal
  Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
awplus(config)# service telnet ip
  % Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated. Please use SSH.
awplus(config)#

  The `% ` line above is a WARNING, not a rejection: the prompt advanced and the command
  took effect.  Read back 24 seconds later:

awplus# show telnet
  Telnet Server Configuration
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Telnet server                      : Enabled
  Protocol                           : IPv4
  Port                               : 23
awplus# show running-config | include telnet
  service telnet
  no service telnet ipv6

  The DUT recorded the same warning itself:
  2026 Oct 19 22:45:02 user.warning awplus IMI[903]: Warning: Telnet is insecure and
  deprecated. Please use SSH.

  A product fact worth recording.  The IPv4-only state is stored as the generic
  `service telnet` PLUS an explicit `no service telnet ipv6`.  It is NOT stored as
  `service telnet ip`.  With both protocols on, only `service telnet` is stored (STEP 5).
  So the running-config tells the three states apart by which negation is present.  Read
  a `| include telnet` grep with that in mind.
  => STEP 2 PASS: the IPv4-only form of the command is accepted and the server reports
     itself Enabled with Protocol IPv4 on port 23.

STEP 3 -- a real authenticated telnet session (designed by the tester)     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: log in over telnet from the box as `manager` and run three commands.  Capture
  the whole conversation on eth3.
  Expected: the client reaches a login prompt, authenticates, and sees real command
  output.  The DUT must list the session as a vty line from the box's address.  The whole
  exchange must appear as one ESTABLISHED TCP/23 conversation carrying cleartext.

