     wire, and the DUT's log and `show users`.  This is the telnet server working.

STEP 4 -- control, a bad credential is refused (designed by the tester)    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: with the server on, try a username the DUT does not hold.  A NON-EXISTENT user
  was used, not a wrong password for `manager`.  AW+ locks an account after 5 failed
  logins, and swi_a on this bench is already locked out that way.  A failure against an
  unknown user cannot count against `manager`.  The password used is a worthless one,
  `testing123`.
  Expected: the TCP connection is ESTABLISHED, and the login is refused by the
  application.  This separates "the server is listening" from "anything can get in".

box$ tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user ckbogus
       --password testing123 --login-timeout 20 --expect fail

  === telnet 10.38.22.34:23 as ckbogus ===
  --- bytes before the username prompt ---
  awplus login:
  --- bytes between username and password prompt ---
  Password:
  --- bytes after the password was sent (THE LOGIN BANNER) ---

  Login incorrect

  awplus login:
  LOGIN RESULT: UNKNOWN  proto=telnet  user=ckbogus  seconds=2.5

  The DEVICE's answer is clear: `Login incorrect`, then a fresh `login:` prompt.  The
  `UNKNOWN` on the last line is the client tool's own grading, not the DUT's answer.

  On the wire, from the same capture as STEP 3:

  flow 10.38.22.33:57714 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=31  duration=2.463s
       => ESTABLISHED+DATA
     10.38.22.33:57714 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=17  payload=52 B
          flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
     10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57714  pkts=14  payload=94 B
          flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, PSH.ACK.URG, SYN.ACK}
  payload 10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57714 (94 B):
     awplus login: Password:
     Login incorrect

     awplus login:

  Third instrument, the DUT's own log:
  2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 daemon.notice awplus telnetd[30403]: Connection from 10.38.22.33
    received.
  2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 authpriv.notice awplus login: pam_unix(vty0:auth): check pass;
    user unknown
  2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 authpriv.notice awplus login: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication
    failure; logname= uid=0 euid=0 tty=/dev/pts/0 ruser= rhost=10.38.22.33
  2026 Oct 19 22:46:18 authpriv.notice awplus login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM 10.38.22.33 FOR
    (unknown), User not known to the underlying authentication module
  => STEP 4 PASS: the DUT sent 94 B and refused, against 2045 B for the accepted session.
     The TCP connection reached ESTABLISHED, so the server really is listening, and the
     refusal happened in the application.  The log counts the failure against `(unknown)`,
     not against `manager`, which is why a non-existent username was chosen.

STEP 5 -- the "(IPv4)" in the title is a real control (designed by tester) PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: turn the IPv6 half on, then remove the IPv4 half alone, then put it back.  Try
  the same IPv4 login at each stage.
  Expected: the two halves are controlled separately.  Turning IPv4 off must stop IPv4
  connections even while the server as a whole still reads Enabled.  No `show` command
  can grade this step; only the wire can.

  5a. Add IPv6 (11:47:40):

awplus(config)# service telnet ipv6
  % Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated. Please use SSH.
awplus# show telnet
  Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4,IPv6 / Port : 23
awplus# show running-config | include telnet
  service telnet

  5b. Remove the IPv4 half only, leaving IPv6 (11:48:10):

awplus(config)# no service telnet ip
awplus# show telnet
  Telnet Server Configuration
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Telnet server                      : Enabled
  Protocol                           : IPv6
  Port                               : 23
awplus# show running-config | include telnet
  service telnet
  no service telnet ip

box$ tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager
       --password friend --login-timeout 10
  -> TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.34:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused  (exit 4)

  on the wire (t33378-v6only.pcap):
  flow 10.38.22.33:57716 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=2  => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
     10.38.22.33:57716 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
     10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57716  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}
                                          <-- `show telnet` says Enabled at this moment,
                                              and IPv4 is refused at the same moment

  5c. Put IPv4 back and take IPv6 away again (11:48:4x):

awplus(config)# service telnet ip
awplus(config)# no service telnet ipv6
awplus# show telnet
  Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4 / Port : 23
awplus# show running-config | include telnet
  service telnet
  no service telnet ipv6

box$ tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager
       --password friend --login-timeout 15 --cmd "show telnet"
  -> LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=telnet  user=manager  seconds=0.2
     Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4 / Port : 23
  on the wire (t33378-v6only.pcap, same window):
  flow 10.38.22.33:57718 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=49  duration=0.812s
       => ESTABLISHED+DATA then RST
     10.38.22.33:57718 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=27  payload=83 B
     10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57718  pkts=22  payload=519 B
  => STEP 5 PASS: the IPv4 and IPv6 halves are separate.  With IPv6 only, the same client
     at the same address was refused on the wire while `show telnet` still read Enabled.
     One configuration line later the same client logged in again.  This is why the case
     is graded on the wire: a run graded from `show telnet` could have passed with the
     IPv4 half switched off.  The IPv6 half was not tested on the wire, because the DUT's
     SVIs have no IPv6 address.

